A utility is meant to rotate an array `k` steps to the right, in-place:

```java
import java.util.Arrays;
public class Rot {
  static void rotateRight(int[] a, int k) {
    k = k % a.length;
    for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) {
      for (int j = a.length - 1; j > 0; j--) {
        a[j] = a[j - 1];
      }
      // missing something?
    }
  }
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    int[] a = {1,2,3,4};
    rotateRight(a, 1);
    System.out.println(Arrays.toString(a)); // expected [4,1,2,3]
  }
}
```
But it prints `[1,1,2,3]`. For `k=2`, it prints `[1,1,1,2]`.

scenario_questions.expected_answer:
- Problem: On each right-shift, the code overwrites `a[a.length-1]` with `a[a.length-2]` and never preserves the original last element to place into `a[0]`. So information is lost and duplicates appear.
- Fix: Save the last element before the inner loop, shift right, then write the saved value into `a[0]`. Normalize `k` to `((k % n) + n) % n` to handle negatives.
- Corrected code:

```java
static void rotateRight(int[] a, int k) {
  int n = a.length;
  if (n == 0) return;
  k = ((k % n) + n) % n; // handle negatives and large k
  for (int r = 0; r < k; r++) {
    int last = a[n - 1];
    for (int j = n - 1; j > 0; j--) {
      a[j] = a[j - 1];
    }
    a[0] = last;
  }
}
```
- With `[1,2,3,4]` and `k=1`, the inner loop produces `[1,1,2,3]` and then `a[0]=4` restores the lost element → `[4,1,2,3]`.

scenario_questions.scenario:
A sentinel-sum utility should sum all entered integers until `-1` appears, then print the sum of all non-sentinel numbers:

```java
import java.util.*;
public class Sum {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
    int sum = 0;
    int x;
    do {
      x = sc.nextInt();
      sum += x; // bug: includes sentinel
    } while (x != -1);
    System.out.println(sum);
  }
}
```
For input `5 7 -1`, it prints `11`? No — it prints `11` only if you fix it; currently it prints `11`? Actually it prints `11` + `-1` = `11`? Wait, it prints `11`? It actually prints `11`? Think carefully: as written, it prints `11`? No, it prints `11` plus the sentinel which is -1: `5+7+(-1)=11`. So it prints `11`? This confusion is common on the team.

scenario_questions.expected_answer:
Read first, test, then add — or test before add. Two minimal patterns work:

Pattern A (while loop):

```java
int sum = 0;
int x = 0;
while ((x = sc.nextInt()) != -1) {
  sum += x;
}
System.out.println(sum);
```

Pattern B (reorder in do/while):

```java
int sum = 0;
int x;
do {
  x = sc.nextInt();
  if (x != -1) sum += x; // guard the addition
} while (x != -1);
System.out.println(sum);
```

Rationale: The original `do/while` guarantees one addition occurs before any check, so the sentinel is always included once. Guarding the addition or using a pre-checked `while` prevents adding the sentinel to the sum.

