scenario_questions.scenario:
You are handed a bug report: a user creates several logically identical Person objects and adds them to a HashSet, but duplicates appear and later lookups by an equivalent Person return null. Current code:
```java
import java.util.*;

final class Person {
  private String nationalId; // may change after verification step
  private String name;

  Person(String nationalId, String name) {
    this.nationalId = nationalId; this.name = name;
  }
  public void setNationalId(String nationalId) { this.nationalId = nationalId; }
  @Override public boolean equals(Object o) {
    return (o instanceof Person p) &&
           Objects.equals(nationalId, p.nationalId);
  }
  // hashCode is NOT overridden
}

Set<Person> people = new HashSet<>();
Person a = new Person(null, "Sam");
people.add(a);
// later, after verification:
a.setNationalId("ID-123");
people.add(new Person("ID-123", "Samuel"));
boolean found = people.contains(new Person("ID-123", "Anyone"));
```
The team asks you to explain the behavior and fix it without breaking future maintenance.

scenario_questions.expected_answer:
- Root cause: equals overridden but hashCode not; HashSet buckets are partitioned by hashCode, so equal objects with unequal hashCodes may live in different buckets. Additionally, mutating a field used in hashCode/equals after adding to a HashSet breaks retrieval because the key's bucket is chosen using an outdated hash, and equals no longer matches within that bucket.
- Fix: override hashCode consistent with equals, e.g. use Objects.hash(nationalId). Make either nationalId immutable or exclude mutable fields from equality. Prefer making nationalId final and set at construction only; if it must change, do not use it as a hash/equality key while mutable, or remove and re-add after change.
- If mutability is required: use a Map keyed by an immutable identifier (e.g., temporary surrogate key) and store Person as a value; or use identity-based collections if logical equality cannot be stable. Alternatively, avoid using Person itself as a key in hashed collections when its equality basis can change.
- Show corrected code:
```java
final class Person {
  private final String nationalId; // make immutable if possible
  private String name;
  Person(String nationalId, String name) { this.nationalId = nationalId; this.name = name; }
  @Override public boolean equals(Object o) {
    return (o instanceof Person p) && Objects.equals(nationalId, p.nationalId);
  }
  @Override public int hashCode() { return Objects.hash(nationalId); }
}
```
- If id cannot be final: document that any change requires remove-then-reinsert, or better, use Map<String,Person> by id and keep Person equality by identity only (equals == reference) to avoid contract violations.

scenario_questions.scenario:
You need a task scheduler that processes tasks by priority but preserves submission order among tasks with the same priority. A first attempt uses:
```java
class Task { final int priority; final String id; Task(int p, String id){ this.priority=p; this.id=id; } }
Queue<Task> q = new PriorityQueue<>(Comparator.comparingInt(t -> t.priority));
```
In testing, tasks with the same priority are not processed in submission order.

scenario_questions.expected_answer:
- PriorityQueue does not guarantee stability among equal-priority elements; tie order is unspecified.
- Design: Wrap Task with a monotonically increasing sequence number captured at enqueue time, and use a comparator that sorts by (priority asc, sequence asc). Keep Queue<TaskWrapper> as a PriorityQueue for O(log n) ops.
- Code:
```java
final class Task { final int priority; final String id; Task(int p, String id){ this.priority=p; this.id=id; } }
final class TaskWrapper {
  final long seq; final Task task;
  TaskWrapper(long seq, Task task){ this.seq=seq; this.task=task; }
}
final class StableScheduler {
  private final PriorityQueue<TaskWrapper> pq = new PriorityQueue<>(
    Comparator.<TaskWrapper>comparingInt(tw -> tw.task.priority)
              .thenComparingLong(tw -> tw.seq)
  );
  private long nextSeq = 0L;
  public void enqueue(Task t){ pq.add(new TaskWrapper(nextSeq++, t)); }
  public Task dequeue(){ return pq.remove().task; }
  public boolean isEmpty(){ return pq.isEmpty(); }
}
```
- Generics: The queue type is PriorityQueue<TaskWrapper>; no raw types. API is type safe.
- Alternatives considered: TreeSet cannot handle true duplicates (equal comparator keys) without additional tie-breaker in the key; LinkedHashMap keyed by priority to a Queue preserves insertion order within each priority but complicates dequeue selection (need to pick the smallest non-empty key efficiently). The wrapper approach keeps operations O(log n) and simple.
- Enqueue/dequeue usage:
```java
StableScheduler s = new StableScheduler();
s.enqueue(new Task(5, "A"));
s.enqueue(new Task(5, "B"));
s.enqueue(new Task(1, "C"));
System.out.println(s.dequeue().id); // C
System.out.println(s.dequeue().id); // A
System.out.println(s.dequeue().id); // B
```

- Failure reason: Java generics are invariant; List<Integer> is not a subtype of List<Number>, so a single T cannot represent both Integer and Number at the same time.
- Correct signature uses PECS (Producer Extends, Consumer Super):
```java
static <T> void copy(List<? super T> dst, List<? extends T> src) {
  for (T t : src) dst.add(t);
}
```
- Example usage:
```java
List<Number> nums = new ArrayList<>();
List<Integer> ints = List.of(1,2,3);
copy(nums, ints);           // OK: T=Integer
List<Object> objs = new ArrayList<>();
copy(objs, nums);           // OK: T=Number
```
- Justification: Source produces T values safely if it's ? extends T; destination consumes T values safely if it's ? super T. This maintains type safety without casts.

scenario_questions.scenario:
You are integrating multiple logging traits into a class hierarchy:
```java
interface LogA { default String tag() { return "A"; } }
interface LogB { default String tag() { return "B"; } }
abstract class Base { public String tag() { return "Base"; } }
class Impl extends Base implements LogA, LogB { }
```
A teammate is unsure what Impl.tag() should return and how to intentionally select a specific behavior. They also want to keep a registry of handlers by their implemented interface to preserve registration order and allow multiple handlers per interface.

scenario_questions.expected_answer:
- Resolution: Class methods beat interface defaults. Since Base provides a concrete tag(), Impl inherits Base.tag(), so new Impl().tag() returns "Base".
- Explicit selection:
```java
class Impl extends Base implements LogA, LogB {
  @Override public String tag() {
    // choose LogA explicitly, or combine with Base if desired
    // return LogA.super.tag();
    return LogA.super.tag() + ":" + super.tag(); // example composition
  }
}
```
- Registry design: Use a LinkedHashMap<Class<?>, List<Object>> to preserve key insertion order and a List to keep handler insertion order and allow duplicates. With generics, expose type-safe methods using wildcards.
```java
class HandlerRegistry {
  private final Map<Class<?>, List<Object>> byIface = new LinkedHashMap<>();
  public <T> void register(Class<T> iface, T handler) {
    byIface.computeIfAbsent(iface, k -> new ArrayList<>()).add(handler);
  }
  @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
  public <T> List<T> getHandlers(Class<T> iface) {
    return (List<T>) byIface.getOrDefault(iface, List.of());
  }
}
```
- Justification: LinkedHashMap preserves insertion order of interface keys; ArrayList preserves registration order and allows duplicates. Wildcards and generic methods provide type safety at API boundaries while storing as Object internally.

