scenario_questions.expected_answer:
A robust design opens one file at a time in a bounded manner and closes it deterministically. Iterate files with `Files.walk` in try-with-resources; for each file, use another try-with-resources around `Files.lines(p)` so the stream closes even on exceptions. Example sketch:
```java
try (Stream<Path> files = Files.walk(root)) {
  files.filter(Files::isRegularFile)
       .forEach(p -> {
         try (Stream<String> lines = Files.lines(p)) {
           final java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicLong lineNo = new java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicLong(0);
           lines.forEach(line -> {
             long n = lineNo.incrementAndGet();
             try {
               Event e = parse(line); // may throw
               // accumulate via thread-safe collector or write to concurrent map
             } catch (RuntimeException ex) {
               logger.log(Level.WARNING, () -> p + ":" + n + " parse failed: " + ex.getMessage());
               // optionally count and continue
             }
           });
         } catch (IOException ioe) {
           logger.log(Level.WARNING, p + " read failed", ioe);
         }
       });
}
```
Concurrency: parallelize across files, not across lines, to keep log order per file and limit open descriptors. Use `files.parallel().forEach(...)` only with a custom `ForkJoinPool` of limited size or use a `Semaphore` to cap concurrently open files to avoid FD exhaustion. Avoid `flatMap` on unclosed streams. Summary is reduced using concurrent collectors (e.g., `Collectors.toConcurrentMap`) or by per-file local maps merged at the end. This eliminates leaks (every `Stream` in try-with-resources), keeps processing despite bad lines (exceptions handled inside the per-line loop), and logs filename:line for observability.

Use `ZonedDateTime` in the target zone and zone rules for resolving gaps/overlaps. Policy: on spring-forward (02:30 nonexistent), run at 03:30 (next valid time); on fall-back (02:30 ambiguous), pick the earlier offset occurrence (first 02:30, e.g., CEST). Code:
```java
ZoneId zone = ZoneId.of("Europe/Berlin");
ZonedDateTime nextLocal = lastRun.plusDays(1).withHour(2).withMinute(30).withSecond(0).withNano(0);
// Resolve gap/overlap deterministically
java.time.zone.ZoneRules rules = zone.getRules();
LocalDateTime ldt = nextLocal.toLocalDateTime();
java.util.List<ZoneOffset> offs = rules.getValidOffsets(ldt);
ZonedDateTime nextRun;
if (offs.isEmpty()) { // gap -> shift forward to next valid time
    ZoneOffsetTransition tr = rules.nextTransition(nextLocal.toInstant());
    nextRun = tr.getDateTimeAfter().withHour(3).withMinute(30); // or rules.getTransition(ldt).getDateTimeAfter()
} else {
    // overlap -> choose earlier offset occurrence (first 02:30)
    nextRun = ZonedDateTime.of(ldt, offs.get(0), zone);
}
```
Alternatively, `ldt.atZone(zone)` already advances into the gap (spring) to 03:30 and in overlap picks the earlier offset; document this policy and assert with tests. Avoid UTC arithmetic for a local-time SLA; always compute in the target zone, then convert to Instant if needed.

scenario_questions.scenario:
Your library has hot-path debug logging like:
```java
if (logger.isLoggable(Level.FINE)) {
    logger.fine(String.format("Scored %f for %s", expensiveScore(obj), obj.getId()));
}
```
Users complain about overhead even when FINE is disabled. They also want exceptions logged with stack traces and to control output via their app’s handlers, not yours.

scenario_questions.expected_answer:
Use lazy message suppliers to defer formatting and computation, and never install global handlers in a library. For exceptions, pass the `Throwable` parameter. Example:
```java
// In library code
logger.log(Level.FINE, () -> String.format("Scored %.3f for %s", expensiveScore(obj), obj.getId()));
// Or avoid String.format allocation entirely:
logger.log(Level.FINE, () -> "Scored " + expensiveScore(obj) + " for " + obj.getId());

try { /* ... */ }
catch (IOException e) {
    logger.log(Level.WARNING, "I/O while loading profile " + id, e);
}
```
Library configuration: obtain loggers with stable names (e.g., `Logger.getLogger("com.example.lib")`), set no handlers and keep `useParentHandlers=true` so the application controls handlers/formatters and levels. Optionally document recommended logger names and levels. This avoids eager formatting and lets apps route output; exception stack traces are emitted by passing the Throwable, not by embedding `e.toString()` in the message.

Strategy:
- Prefer atomic move with replace when supported; on failure, fallback to copy-then-atomic-rename in target dir.
- Preserve attributes when copying.
- Retry on transient `AccessDeniedException` with bounded backoff; give up after N attempts and continue with other files.
- Skip files recently modified (age threshold) to avoid ingesting active files.
- Log each outcome at appropriate level.
Code sketch:
```java
Path src = ...; Path dst = archive.resolve(src.getFileName());
if (Files.getLastModifiedTime(src).toMillis() > System.currentTimeMillis() - ageMs) {
    logger.fine(() -> "Skipping active file " + src);
    return;
}
try {
    Files.move(src, dst, StandardCopyOption.ATOMIC_MOVE, StandardCopyOption.REPLACE_EXISTING);
} catch (AtomicMoveNotSupportedException e) {
    Path tmp = dst.resolveSibling(dst.getFileName() + ".part");
    Files.copy(src, tmp, StandardCopyOption.REPLACE_EXISTING, StandardCopyOption.COPY_ATTRIBUTES);
    Files.move(tmp, dst, StandardCopyOption.ATOMIC_MOVE, StandardCopyOption.REPLACE_EXISTING);
    Files.delete(src);
} catch (AccessDeniedException e) {
    int attempts = 0;
    while (attempts++ < maxRetries) {
        try { Thread.sleep(backoff(attempts)); Files.move(src, dst, StandardCopyOption.REPLACE_EXISTING); return; }
        catch (AccessDeniedException ignored) { /* retry */ }
    }
    logger.log(Level.WARNING, "Lock persisted for " + src + ", giving up", e);
}
```
Justification: `ATOMIC_MOVE` prevents readers of `archive/` from ever seeing a partial file; copy+atomic-rename emulates this across filesystems. COPY_ATTRIBUTES preserves timestamps/permissions. The age check reduces collisions with writers. Retries handle transient scanner locks without stalling the whole batch; failures are logged and the loop proceeds.

