scenario_questions.scenario:
A funds-transfer service currently performs two balance updates and then writes an audit row, all in one JDBC method. Occasionally the audit insert fails due to a foreign key mismatch, leaving the balances moved but no audit row. The code resembles:

```java
c.setAutoCommit(false);
try {
  debit(fromId, amt, c);
  credit(toId, amt, c);
  insertAudit(txId, fromId, toId, amt, c); // can throw SQLException
  c.commit();
} catch (SQLException e) {
  // log only
}
```
The business rule is "transfer is atomic with audit" — either both balances change and an audit row exists, or nothing changes. The service handles 200 TPS and must not serialize all transfers globally.

scenario_questions.expected_answer:
Use a single transaction per transfer and roll back the entire transaction on any failure, including audit. Remove the outer commit in the catch block and add an explicit rollback. Optional: keep a single savepoint before starting any writes if additional operations follow, but here a full transaction rollback suffices. Pseudocode:

```java
c.setAutoCommit(false);
try {
  debit(...);
  credit(...);
  insertAudit(...);
  c.commit();
} catch (SQLException e) {
  try { c.rollback(); } catch (SQLException suppressed) { /* log */ }
  // log error with txId and account ids for traceability
  // propagate a domain exception so caller sees the transfer failed atomically
  throw new TransferFailedException(e);
}
```
Maintain row-level lock scope by updating accounts in a consistent order (e.g., lower id first) to reduce deadlock risk at 200 TPS, not by serializing the whole table. Isolation READ_COMMITTED is sufficient for this write path if business rules tolerate concurrent reads; do not elevate to SERIALIZABLE. Ensure the audit insert uses the same connection so it participates in the transaction. Log sufficient context (txId, fromId, toId, amount, SQLState) but no sensitive data. Return an error to the caller so they can retry safely, since no partial state is committed.

scenario_questions.scenario:
Your database tests are flaky in CI. Sometimes test A passes locally but fails in CI because rows from a previous test remain. The repository class accepts a Connection created from DriverManager in production code, and tests connect to the same RDBMS as production. Current test:

```java
class OrderRepoTest {
  static Connection c;
  @BeforeAll static void open() throws Exception { c = DriverManager.getConnection(url); }
  @AfterAll static void close() throws Exception { c.close(); }

  @Test void createOrders() throws Exception {
    // creates schema if absent, then inserts 2 rows
  }

  @Test void findOrders() throws Exception {
    // expects 0 rows initially, then inserts and queries
  }
}
```
CI parallelizes tests by class; your suite may also run in random order.

scenario_questions.expected_answer:
Use an isolated database per test class (ideally per test) with an in-memory engine such as H2 or a temporary file DB. Refactor repository to accept a DataSource so tests can inject an H2 DataSource while production uses the real one. Manage lifecycle with @BeforeEach/@AfterEach to ensure clean state. Option A: wrap each test in a transaction and roll back in @AfterEach. Option B: recreate schema/data in @BeforeEach and drop in @AfterEach.

Concrete steps:
- Change repo constructors to take javax.sql.DataSource.
- Test code:
```java
@TestInstance(PER_CLASS)
class OrderRepoTest {
  DataSource ds;
  @BeforeAll void init() { ds = new org.h2.jdbcx.JdbcDataSource(); /* url=mem:db;DB_CLOSE_DELAY=-1 */ }
  @BeforeEach void reset() throws SQLException { try (Connection c = ds.getConnection(); Statement s=c.createStatement()){ s.execute("DROP ALL OBJECTS"); s.execute(schemaSql); seedIfNeeded(); } }
  @Test void createOrders() { /* ... use ds */ }
}
```
- Configure Surefire to limit parallelism or disable parallel for DB tests; or mark DB tests in a group and run them sequentially if necessary.
- Maven: ensure H2 is a test-scoped dependency and surefire version supports JUnit 5.
- Avoid static shared Connection; acquire/close per test to prevent cross-test leakage.
